<section class="section" aria-labelledby="pvc-business-cards">
  <p class="eyebrow">A LASTING IMPRESSION. A MODERN CONNECTION.</p>

  <h2 id="pvc-business-cards">
    More than a printed introduction.
    <br />
    <em>Designed to stay connected.</em>
  </h2>

  <p>
    Your business card is often the first piece of your brand someone takes
    away. It should feel professional, communicate clearly, and make it easy
    to reconnect. PVC business cards combine a durable material with a design
    that can be clean, distinctive, and practical, giving your business an
    introduction worth keeping.
  </p>

  <p>
    Unlike traditional paper cards, PVC cards resist water and everyday
    handling, helping them stay presentable through repeated use. Their sturdy
    feel adds substance to your design, while your logo, colours, and typography
    make the card unmistakably yours. Although durable, PVC is not
    indestructible: scratching, excessive heat, and bending can still affect
    its appearance.
  </p>

  <h3>A modern look with a practical purpose.</h3>

  <p>
    Adding a QR code brings another dimension to your card. Integrated
    thoughtfully into the layout, it becomes part of the design rather than
    an afterthought. A clear arrangement, strong contrast, and a simple
    invitation such as <strong>“Scan to save my details”</strong> give the
    code both visual purpose and practical value.
  </p>

  <p>
    With a compatible smartphone camera or QR scanner, the recipient can
    access the encoded information without manually typing each detail.
    It is a convenient connection between your printed card and the
    information you want someone to keep.
  </p>

  <h3>Your details, directly inside the QR code.</h3>

  <p>
    A QR code does not have to send someone to a website. We can encode
    supported contact information directly into the code itself, using a format such as <strong>vCard</strong>. Compatible scanners can recognise
    this information and may offer the recipient an option to save it
    as a contact.
  </p>

  <p>Your QR code can include information such as:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Your name and business name.</li>
    <li>Your telephone number and email address.</li>
    <li>Your business address and website address.</li>
    <li>Alternatively, a short plain-text message.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Reading directly encoded information can work without an internet
    connection. However, opening a website, sending an email, or using an
    online service still requires the appropriate connectivity. The available
    actions depend on the phone, scanner, and information format.
  </p>

  <h3>Give every introduction somewhere to go.</h3>

  <p>
    If you prefer an online experience, your QR code can instead link to a
    portfolio, product catalogue, booking page, or business website. This
    creates a natural conversation starter:
    <strong>“Scan here and take a look at what we can create.”</strong>
    Choose the destination that makes the most sense for your business
    and the people you meet.
  </p>

  <p>
    Whatever you share, keep it focused. More encoded data creates a denser
    QR code, which can be harder to scan at small sizes. A clear surrounding
    margin and testing at the final print size help ensure usability.
    Visible contact details keep your card useful for people who prefer
    not to scan.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>
      A PVC business card combines a lasting physical introduction with the
      convenience of a scan, helping your brand look professional and making
      the next connection easier.
    </strong>
  </p>

  <p class="fine">
    QR compatibility varies by device, scanner, and data format. Changing
    directly encoded details requires a new QR code and reprinting. Content on a webpage you control can be updated without reprinting, provided
    the encoded web address stays the same.
  </p>

